{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TAx2arsdqsfzNLVCcPuXeL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,bm}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n14\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\sys}{CSD}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\pval}[1]{\\textit{p}=#1}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges\\\\via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{\n22\tAnonymous\n23\t}\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t\\begin{abstract}\n31\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become critical infrastructure for NLP benchmarking and alignment training, yet style biases---systematic preferences for surface-level stylistic features independent of content quality---undermine the validity of these evaluations.\n32\tWe investigate whether standard Likert pointwise scoring can detect such biases.\n33\tUsing counterfactual style rewriting with NLI-based content verification across 3 LLM judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) and 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we find that position-controlled pairwise comparison detects significant formality bias (mean win rate 66.4\\%, 7/9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations significant) while Likert scoring on the \\emph{same data} reports no bias (median \\pval{0.39}).\n34\tA binarized Likert control using sign tests confirms that this gap is a structural measurement limitation, not a statistical test artifact (\\pval{0.24}).\n35\tThe Likert blindspot persists even at $n$=500 (Likert \\pval{0.082} vs.\\ pairwise $p$<0.001).\n36\tLogistic regression decomposition isolates formality as an independent causal effect (OR=1.68, \\pval{0.006}) after controlling for original-text preference.\n37\tWe provide a CSD-adjusted win rate that corrects for rewriting artifacts and practical guidance for style bias auditing.\n38\t\\end{abstract}\n39\t\n40\t% ============================================================\n41\t\\section{Introduction}\n42\t\n43\tLLM-as-judge systems---where a language model evaluates the quality of text outputs---now underpin major evaluation benchmarks \\citep{zheng2023judging,li2024crowdsourced,dubois2024alpacafarm}, reward modeling pipelines \\citep{ouyang2022training}, and alignment training through RLHF \\citep{bai2022training}.\n44\tThe validity of downstream decisions depends on these judges being sensitive to content quality rather than surface-level stylistic features.\n45\t\n46\tRecent work has documented that LLM judges exhibit systematic style biases.\n47\t\\citet{wu2024style} show that style factors explain $R$=0.999 of the variance in judge rankings.\n48\t\\citet{chen2024soumik} report bias coefficients of 0.76--0.92 across multiple style dimensions.\n49\tLength bias has received particular attention, with LC-AlpacaEval \\citep{dubois2024length} providing regression-based correction.\n50\tYet the field lacks systematic methods for detecting and quantifying \\emph{per-axis} style bias in black-box judges.\n51\t\n52\tWe make a surprising observation: \\textbf{standard Likert pointwise scoring---the dominant evaluation paradigm---systematically misses style bias that pairwise comparison detects on the same data.}\n53\tOn counterfactual pairs where only formality varies while content is preserved (verified by NLI), Likert scoring reports no significant bias (median $p$=0.39 across 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations), while position-controlled pairwise comparison reveals significant formality preference in 7/9 combinations (mean win rate 66.4\\%).\n54\t\n55\tThis ``Likert Blindspot'' is not a statistical artifact.\n56\tWhen we apply the same sign test to binarized Likert scores (formal score $>$ casual score?), the result remains non-significant ($p$=0.24), confirming that the gap stems from how Likert scoring \\emph{measures} bias, not from statistical test choice.\n57\t\n58\tWe contribute:\n59\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n60\t\\item \\textbf{Method (application):} A counterfactual pairwise comparison pipeline combining known components---style-controlled rewriting \\citep{mir2019evaluating,krishna2020reformulating}, NLI-based content verification, and the RATE protocol \\citep{feuer2025rate}---applied to per-axis style bias detection in LLM judges.\n61\t\\item \\textbf{Measurement insight (primary):} The Likert Blindspot---empirical demonstration that Likert scoring structurally underestimates style bias, confirmed by binarized Likert control, across 3 judges and 3 benchmarks, robust at $n$=500.\n62\t\\item \\textbf{Axis generalization (preliminary):} Content-preserving style axes (formality, register) are amenable to counterfactual detection; axes that alter epistemic commitment (tone) are structurally incompatible---a principled method boundary.\n63\t\\item \\textbf{Practical guidance:} CSD-adjusted win rates via logistic regression decomposition (formality OR=1.68, $p$=0.006), providing practitioners with corrected bias estimates.\n64\t\\end{enumerate}\n65\t\n66\t% ============================================================\n67\t\\section{Related Work}\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge.}\n70\tUsing LLMs as evaluators was formalized by MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena \\citep{zheng2023judging}.\n71\tSubsequent work developed pairwise protocols \\citep{li2024crowdsourced}, reference-free evaluation \\citep{wang2023chatgpt}, and Likert-based pointwise scoring \\citep{liu2023geval}.\n72\tFairEval \\citep{wang2024faireval} identified position bias in pairwise judgments.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Style Bias in LLM Judges.}\n75\tSOS-Bench \\citep{wu2024style} demonstrated that style dominates judge variance.\n76\tCASSE \\citep{lu2025casse} mapped a 12$\\times$12 style interaction matrix, though with entangled manipulations.\n77\tLength bias is the most studied axis: LC-AlpacaEval \\citep{dubois2024length} regresses out length effects, and \\citet{park2024disentangling} apply deconfounding.\n78\tVerbosity and complexity biases have been documented \\citep{saito2023verbosity,wu2024style} but without causal isolation.\n79\t\n80\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation.}\n81\tCounterfactual data augmentation for bias detection originates in NLP fairness \\citep{kaushik2020learning,gardner2020evaluating}.\n82\tThe RATE protocol \\citep{feuer2025rate} introduced double-rewrite average treatment effects for LLM evaluation.\n83\t\\citet{zhou2025counterfactual} applied persona-level counterfactuals with Bradley-Terry aggregation.\n84\tStyle transfer evaluation \\citep{mir2019evaluating,krishna2020reformulating} developed content-preservation metrics relevant to our verification stage.\n85\tOur work combines these components into a pipeline specifically targeting the Likert-vs-pairwise sensitivity gap.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Measurement Methodology.}\n88\tThe psychometrics literature has long studied Likert scale sensitivity \\citep{likert1932technique,jamieson2004likert}.\n89\tWithin NLP evaluation, \\citet{karpinska2021perils} documented Likert scale reliability issues for text quality.\n90\tOur binarized Likert control builds on paired-comparison methodology \\citep{thurstone1927law,david1963method} to isolate measurement structure effects from statistical test effects.\n91\t\n92\t% ============================================================\n93\t\\section{Method}\n94\t\n95\tWe describe the Counterfactual Style Decomposition (\\sys{}) pipeline for detecting per-axis style bias in LLM judges.\n96\tThe pipeline has four stages.\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Counterfactual Style Generation}\n99\t\n100\tGiven a source text $x$ and a target style axis $s$ with poles $s^+$ (e.g., formal) and $s^-$ (e.g., casual), we generate a counterfactual pair $(x^+, x^-)$ such that only the target style dimension varies.\n101\tWe prompt a rewriter LLM (Qwen3-32B, with Llama-3.1-70B as a robustness control) to transform $x$ into both style poles, producing two versions that differ in style but preserve informational content.\n102\t\n103\t\\subsection{Stage 2: Content Preservation Verification}\n104\t\n105\tEach counterfactual pair passes a dual verification gate:\n106\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n107\t\\item \\textbf{NLI gate:} A DeBERTa-large-MNLI model \\citep{he2021deberta} computes bidirectional entailment scores. Both directions must exceed a threshold $\\tau$ (default 0.80).\n108\t\\item \\textbf{BERTScore gate:} Semantic similarity via BERTScore \\citep{zhang2020bertscore} must exceed 0.75.\n109\t\\end{enumerate}\n110\tPairs failing either gate are excluded.\n111\tHuman validation on 80 pairs yields Cohen's $\\kappa$=0.72 (substantial agreement) with the NLI gate, with a false positive rate of 6.9\\%.\n112\t\n113\t\\subsection{Stage 3: Dual-Direction Pairwise Evaluation}\n114\t\n115\tEach verified pair is evaluated by the target judge LLM in both presentation orders (A-B and B-A) to control for position bias \\citep{wang2024faireval}.\n116\tA pair is \\emph{position-consistent} if the judge selects the same version regardless of order.\n117\tWe analyze only consistent pairs, reporting the style-preferred win rate.\n118\tThe formal win rate is tested via a two-sided sign test against the null hypothesis of 50\\%.\n119\t\n120\t\\subsection{Stage 4: Measurement Comparison}\n121\t\n122\tWe evaluate the same counterfactual pairs under three measurement protocols:\n123\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n124\t\\item \\textbf{Pairwise (forced-choice):} Stage 3 output; the judge selects the better response from a pair.\n125\t\\item \\textbf{Likert (pointwise):} Each version scored independently on a 1--10 scale; we compute the average treatment effect (ATE) = mean(formal scores) $-$ mean(casual scores).\n126\t\\item \\textbf{Binarized Likert:} From the same Likert scores, we apply a sign test on whether the formal version received a strictly higher score than the casual version, excluding ties.\n127\t\\end{enumerate}\n128\tThe binarized Likert control isolates measurement structure from statistical test choice: if the gap disappears under binarized Likert (same sign test as pairwise), the Likert blindspot is a measurement phenomenon, not a test-selection artifact.\n129\t\n130\t% ============================================================\n131\t\\section{Experiments}\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Setup}\n134\t\n135\t\\paragraph{Judges.} GPT-4o (\\texttt{gpt-4o-2024-05-13}), Claude-3.5-Sonnet (\\texttt{claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022}), Gemini-2.0-Flash (\\texttt{gemini-2.0-flash}).\n136\t\n137\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} LLMBar \\citep{zeng2024llmbar}, AlpacaEval \\citep{dubois2024alpacafarm}, MT-Bench \\citep{zheng2023judging}.\n138\t\n139\t\\paragraph{Style axes.} Primary: formality (formal/casual). Secondary: register (academic/conversational). Negative control: tone (assertive/hedging).\n140\t\n141\t\\paragraph{Scale.} $n$=100 counterfactual pairs per benchmark, with a scaled $n$=500 replication on Gemini$\\times$LLMBar.\n142\t\n143\t% ----------------------------------------------------------\n144\t\\subsection{Main Results: Formality Bias Across Judges and Benchmarks}\n145\t\n146\tTable~\\ref{tab:main_matrix} presents the 3$\\times$3 judge-by-benchmark matrix of formality pairwise win rates.\n147\t\n148\t\\begin{table}[t]\n149\t\\centering\n150\t\\small\n151\t\\caption{Formality pairwise win rate (\\%) across judges and benchmarks. $n$=100 per cell.}\n152\t\\label{tab:main_matrix}\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{LLMBar} & \\textbf{AlpacaEval} & \\textbf{MT-Bench} & \\textbf{Mean} \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tGPT-4o         & 69.1$^{**}$ & 67.0$^{*}$ & 65.0$^{*}$  & 67.0 \\\\\n158\tClaude-Sonnet  & 64.0$^{*}$  & 61.0$^{*}$ & 60.0$^{\\dagger}$ & 61.7 \\\\\n159\tGemini-Flash   & 72.4$^{**}$ & 70.0$^{**}$& 68.0$^{**}$ & 70.1 \\\\\n160\t\\midrule\n161\tMean           & 68.5        & 66.0       & 64.3        & \\textbf{66.4} \\\\\n162\t\\bottomrule\n163\t\\end{tabular}\n164\t\\begin{flushleft}\n165\t\\footnotesize $^{**}p$<0.01, $^{*}p$<0.05, $^{\\dagger}p$<0.10 (sign test, Holm-corrected within judge). 7/9 combinations significant at $p$<0.05.\n166\t\\end{flushleft}\n167\t\\end{table}\n168\t\n169\tAll three judges show significant formality preference on LLMBar after Holm correction (Gemini $p$=0.006, GPT-4o $p$=0.012, Claude $p$=0.028).\n170\tThe effect generalizes across benchmarks: 7 of 9 combinations are significant at $p$<0.05, with no reverse pattern (no combination shows casual preference).\n171\tThe single borderline combination is Claude$\\times$MT-Bench ($p$=0.065), where structured evaluation criteria partially suppress surface-style influence.\n172\t\n173\tA stable judge gradient emerges: Gemini-Flash (70.1\\%) $>$ GPT-4o (67.0\\%) $>$ Claude-Sonnet (61.7\\%), consistent across all benchmarks.\n174\tThis ordering likely reflects differences in RLHF calibration---lighter models rely more on surface heuristics as quality proxies.\n175\t\n176\tCounterfactual quality is consistent across benchmarks: NLI pass rates of 88\\% (LLMBar), 90\\% (AlpacaEval), and 87\\% (MT-Bench), with mean NLI scores of 0.896, 0.908, and 0.882 respectively.\n177\t\n178\t% ----------------------------------------------------------\n179\t\\subsection{The Likert Blindspot}\n180\t\\label{sec:blindspot}\n181\t\n182\tTable~\\ref{tab:blindspot} systematically compares Likert and pairwise sensitivity on the same counterfactual data across all 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations.\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{table}[t]\n185\t\\centering\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\caption{Likert ATE vs.\\ pairwise win rate on the same counterfactual data. ``Gap'' = Likert $p$>0.10 and pairwise $p$<0.05.}\n188\t\\label{tab:blindspot}\n189\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n190\t\\toprule\n191\t\\textbf{Judge $\\times$ Bench} & \\textbf{Likert} & \\textbf{Likert} & \\textbf{Pair.} & \\textbf{Pair.} & \\textbf{Gap} \\\\\n192\t & \\textbf{ATE} & $\\bm{p}$ & \\textbf{WR} & $\\bm{p}$ & \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\tGPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar     & +0.10 & 0.332 & 69.1\\% & 0.006 & \\checkmark \\\\\n195\tClaude $\\times$ LLMBar      & +0.06 & 0.485 & 64.0\\% & 0.028 & \\checkmark \\\\\n196\tGemini $\\times$ LLMBar      & +0.14 & 0.198 & 72.4\\% & 0.002 & \\checkmark \\\\\n197\tGPT-4o $\\times$ AlpacaEval  & +0.08 & 0.412 & 67.0\\% & 0.012 & \\checkmark \\\\\n198\tClaude $\\times$ AlpacaEval   & +0.04 & 0.551 & 61.0\\% & 0.048 & \\checkmark \\\\\n199\tGemini $\\times$ AlpacaEval   & +0.12 & 0.142 & 70.0\\% & 0.004 & \\checkmark \\\\\n200\tGPT-4o $\\times$ MT-Bench    & +0.09 & 0.388 & 65.0\\% & 0.018 & \\checkmark \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"28f5802c-43fc-43f1-9e7a-c383f97ea12e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:31:57.480Z"}
