{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0116Y5Wa1QQXow7ZYRKAGNPg","type":"tool_result","content":"300\t\\begin{flushleft}\n301\t\\footnotesize Mean original preference: 53.0\\%, contributing $\\sim$3 percentage points vs.\\ formality's $\\sim$18.5 points.\n302\t\\end{flushleft}\n303\t\\end{table}\n304\t\n305\tOriginal preference contributes at most $\\sim$3 percentage points (mean 53.0\\%, all $p$>0.28), far smaller than the formality premium of $\\sim$18.5 points.\n306\tIn the casual-original subset---where formality preference opposes original preference---formal versions still win at 63.0\\% (Claude, $p$=0.072) and 61.1\\% (Gemini, $p$=0.096), confirming formality dominates.\n307\t\n308\t\\paragraph{NLI threshold sensitivity.}\n309\tResults hold across the full 70\\%--95\\% NLI threshold range (Table~\\ref{tab:nli_sens}), with win rates monotonically increasing from 69.8\\% to 75.0\\% as content preservation quality increases.\n310\t\n311\t\\begin{table}[t]\n312\t\\centering\n313\t\\small\n314\t\\caption{NLI threshold sensitivity (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar). All thresholds significant.}\n315\t\\label{tab:nli_sens}\n316\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n317\t\\toprule\n318\t\\textbf{Threshold} & $\\bm{n_{\\text{pass}}}$ & $\\bm{n_c}$ & \\textbf{Formal WR} & $\\bm{p}$ \\\\\n319\t\\midrule\n320\t$\\geq$70\\% & 96 & 53 & 69.8\\% & 0.005 \\\\\n321\t$\\geq$80\\% (default) & 88 & 50 & 70.0\\% & 0.005 \\\\\n322\t$\\geq$90\\% & 52 & 46 & 73.9\\% & 0.002 \\\\\n323\t$\\geq$95\\% & 36 & 28 & 75.0\\% & 0.013 \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\t\\paragraph{Rewriter ablation.}\n329\tReplacing Qwen3-32B with Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct yields a consistent effect: 66.0\\% ($p$=0.015) vs.\\ 69.1\\% ($p$=0.006), with no significant difference between rewriters (Fisher $p$=0.42).\n330\t\n331\t\\paragraph{Human NLI validation.}\n332\tManual annotation of 80 counterfactual pairs yields Cohen's $\\kappa$=0.72 with NLI gate decisions.\n333\tThe NLI gate has a low false positive rate (6.9\\%---pairs that pass genuinely preserve content) and a moderate false negative rate (12.9\\%---conservative rejection of valid pairs).\n334\t\n335\t\\paragraph{Consistency filter bias.}\n336\tPosition-consistency filtering (discard rate $\\sim$44\\%) does not introduce systematic bias: retained and discarded pairs show no significant differences in NLI scores ($p$=0.42), text length ($p$=0.58), BERTScore ($p$=0.38), or original style distribution ($p$=0.67).\n337\t\n338\t% ----------------------------------------------------------\n339\t\\subsection{Axis Generalization}\n340\t\n341\t\\paragraph{Register (academic/conversational).}\n342\tRegister---the second content-preserving surface axis---shows significant bias across all three judges on LLMBar (Table~\\ref{tab:register}): GPT-4o 66.0\\% ($p$=0.015), Claude 62.0\\% ($p$=0.042), Gemini 71.4\\% ($p$=0.003).\n343\tThe Likert blindspot extends to register: no Likert ATE is significant (all $p$>0.16).\n344\tThe judge gradient (Gemini $>$ GPT-4o $>$ Claude) is preserved, confirming it is an intrinsic judge property.\n345\t\n346\t\\begin{table}[t]\n347\t\\centering\n348\t\\small\n349\t\\caption{Cross-axis summary (LLMBar, 3 judges).}\n350\t\\label{tab:register}\n351\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n352\t\\toprule\n353\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Mean WR} & \\textbf{Judges Sig.} & \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{Likert?} \\\\\n354\t\\midrule\n355\tFormality  & 68.5\\% & 3/3 & 88\\% & 0/3 sig. \\\\\n356\tRegister   & 66.5\\% & 3/3 & 86\\% & 0/3 sig. \\\\\n357\tTone       & ---    & ---  & 65\\% (fail) & N/A \\\\\n358\t\\bottomrule\n359\t\\end{tabular}\n360\t\\end{table}\n361\t\n362\t\\paragraph{Tone (assertive/hedging)---negative control.}\n363\tTone manipulation alters epistemic commitment (``X is the best'' $\\rightarrow$ ``X might be reasonable''), violating content preservation.\n364\tThe NLI gate correctly rejects this: only 65\\% pass rate (vs.\\ 86--88\\% for formality/register), with 30\\% catastrophic NLI failures ($<$0.01).\n365\tThis establishes a principled method boundary: \\sys{} applies to surface-level style transformations that do not alter informational content or epistemic commitment.\n366\t\n367\t% ----------------------------------------------------------\n368\t\\subsection{Formal Decomposition of Confounds}\n369\t\n370\tWe fit a logistic regression to pooled pairwise data ($N$=459 observations) with formal-preferred as the dependent variable and predictors for formality, original status, their interaction, and judge/benchmark fixed effects (Table~\\ref{tab:logistic}).\n371\t\n372\t\\begin{table}[t]\n373\t\\centering\n374\t\\small\n375\t\\caption{Logistic regression: formality vs.\\ original preference.}\n376\t\\label{tab:logistic}\n377\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n378\t\\toprule\n379\t\\textbf{Predictor} & $\\bm{\\beta}$ & \\textbf{SE} & $\\bm{z}$ & $\\bm{p}$ & \\textbf{OR} \\\\\n380\t\\midrule\n381\tIntercept (formality) & 0.52 & 0.19 & 2.74 & 0.006 & 1.68 \\\\\n382\tis\\_formal\\_original  & 0.34 & 0.17 & 2.00 & 0.046 & 1.40 \\\\\n383\tInteraction            & 0.12 & 0.22 & 0.55 & 0.583 & 1.13 \\\\\n384\tJudge: Claude          & $-$0.28 & 0.17 & $-$1.65 & 0.110 & 0.76 \\\\\n385\tJudge: Gemini          & 0.19 & 0.16 & 1.19 & 0.240 & 1.21 \\\\\n386\tBench: AlpacaEval      & $-$0.08 & 0.16 & $-$0.50 & 0.620 & 0.92 \\\\\n387\tBench: MT-Bench        & $-$0.15 & 0.17 & $-$0.88 & 0.380 & 0.86 \\\\\n388\t\\bottomrule\n389\t\\end{tabular}\n390\t\\begin{flushleft}\n391\t\\footnotesize $N$=459. McFadden $R^2$=0.042. OR = odds ratio.\n392\t\\end{flushleft}\n393\t\\end{table}\n394\t\n395\tThe formality main effect is significant ($\\beta$=0.52, $p$=0.006): controlling for original status, the odds of formal being preferred are 1.68$\\times$ those of casual.\n396\tOriginal preference exists but is weaker (OR=1.40, $p$=0.046), and the interaction is non-significant ($p$=0.58), meaning formality bias operates independently of whether the formal version is the original.\n397\tJudge and benchmark effects are non-significant, confirming the bias is stable across conditions.\n398\t\n399\tThis yields a \\textbf{CSD-adjusted win rate}: the raw mean of 66.4\\% reduces to 63.1\\% (95\\% CI: [57.2\\%, 69.0\\%]) after subtracting the 3.3-point original preference contribution.\n400\tThe adjusted rate remains well above 50\\%, confirming a genuine formality bias of $\\sim$13 percentage points.\n401\t\n402\t% ============================================================\n403\t\\section{Discussion}\n404\t\n405\t\\paragraph{Implications for evaluation practice.}\n406\tThe Likert blindspot has immediate practical consequences: style bias audits using pointwise Likert scoring will systematically underreport bias.\n407\tOur results suggest that any evaluation protocol assessing judge fairness should incorporate pairwise comparison on counterfactual pairs rather than relying solely on Likert ATE.\n408\tThis applies to benchmark design (AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), RLHF reward model validation, and judge selection for deployment.\n409\t\n410\t\\paragraph{Practical recommendations.}\n411\tBased on our findings, we recommend: (1)~use pairwise comparison rather than Likert scoring for style bias auditing; (2)~run RATE controls to quantify original preference; (3)~report both raw and CSD-adjusted win rates; (4)~verify content preservation per axis before applying counterfactual detection---not all style axes are amenable.\n412\t\n413\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n414\tOur study has several limitations.\n415\tFirst, we test only two working style axes (formality, register); the space of possible axes is much larger.\n416\tSecond, counterfactual pairs are generated by LLMs and verified by NLI, introducing possible systematic artifacts despite our controls.\n417\tThird, $n$=100 per cell limits the precision of per-combination estimates, though the $n$=500 replication and pooled logistic regression provide stronger statistical grounding.\n418\tFourth, the CSD-adjusted win rate is a simple correction; a full debiasing system remains future work.\n419\t\n420\t% ============================================================\n421\t\\section{Conclusion}\n422\t\n423\tWe demonstrate that standard Likert scoring systematically fails to detect style bias in LLM judges---a Likert blindspot confirmed across 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and validated by a binarized Likert control.\n424\tCounterfactual pairwise comparison reveals significant formality bias (mean 66.4\\%, 7/9 combinations significant) that Likert scoring entirely misses (median $p$=0.39).\n425\tLogistic regression decomposes the effect, confirming formality as an independent causal factor (OR=1.68, $p$=0.006) after controlling for rewriting artifacts.\n426\tAs LLM-as-judge systems become central infrastructure for AI evaluation and alignment, auditing their style biases with appropriately sensitive measurement instruments is essential.\n427\t\n428\t% ============================================================\n429\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n430\t\n431\t\\begin{thebibliography}{30}\n432\t\n433\t\\bibitem[Bai et~al.(2022)]{bai2022training}\n434\tBai, Y., Jones, A., Ndousse, K., et~al.\n435\t\\newblock Training a helpful and harmless assistant with reinforcement learning from human feedback.\n436\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2204.05862}, 2022.\n437\t\n438\t\\bibitem[Chen et~al.(2026)]{chen2024soumik}\n439\tChen, S., et~al.\n440\t\\newblock Quantifying stylistic bias in LLM-based evaluation.\n441\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2601.xxxxx}, 2026.\n442\t\n443\t\\bibitem[David(1963)]{david1963method}\n444\tDavid, H.~A.\n445\t\\newblock \\emph{The Method of Paired Comparisons}.\n446\t\\newblock Griffin, London, 1963.\n447\t\n448\t\\bibitem[Dubois et~al.(2024a)]{dubois2024alpacafarm}\n449\tDubois, Y., Li, C.~X., Taori, R., et~al.\n450\t\\newblock AlpacaFarm: A simulation framework for methods that learn from human feedback.\n451\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n452\t\n453\t\\bibitem[Dubois et~al.(2024b)]{dubois2024length}\n454\tDubois, Y., Galambosi, B., Liang, P., and Hashimoto, T.~B.\n455\t\\newblock Length-controlled AlpacaEval: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n456\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n457\t\n458\t\\bibitem[Feuer et~al.(2025)]{feuer2025rate}\n459\tFeuer, B., et~al.\n460\t\\newblock {RATE}: Rewrite-based average treatment effect estimation for LLM evaluation.\n461\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2025.\n462\t\n463\t\\bibitem[Gardner et~al.(2020)]{gardner2020evaluating}\n464\tGardner, M., Artzi, Y., Basmov, V., et~al.\n465\t\\newblock Evaluating models' local decision boundaries via contrast sets.\n466\t\\newblock In \\emph{Findings of EMNLP}, 2020.\n467\t\n468\t\\bibitem[He et~al.(2021)]{he2021deberta}\n469\tHe, P., Liu, X., Gao, J., and Chen, W.\n470\t\\newblock {DeBERTa}: Decoding-enhanced {BERT} with disentangled attention.\n471\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n472\t\n473\t\\bibitem[Jamieson(2004)]{jamieson2004likert}\n474\tJamieson, S.\n475\t\\newblock Likert scales: How to (ab)use them.\n476\t\\newblock \\emph{Medical Education}, 38(12):1217--1218, 2004.\n477\t\n478\t\\bibitem[Karpinska et~al.(2021)]{karpinska2021perils}\n479\tKarpinska, M., Arunesh, N., and Iyyer, M.\n480\t\\newblock The perils of using {M}echanical {T}urk to evaluate open-ended text generation.\n481\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2021.\n482\t\n483\t\\bibitem[Kaushik et~al.(2020)]{kaushik2020learning}\n484\tKaushik, D., Hovy, E., and Lipton, Z.~C.\n485\t\\newblock Learning the difference that makes a difference with counterfactually-augmented data.\n486\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2020.\n487\t\n488\t\\bibitem[Krishna et~al.(2020)]{krishna2020reformulating}\n489\tKrishna, K., Wieting, J., and Iyyer, M.\n490\t\\newblock Reformulating unsupervised style transfer as paraphrase generation.\n491\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2020.\n492\t\n493\t\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024crowdsourced}\n494\tLi, T., Chiang, W.-L., Frick, E., et~al.\n495\t\\newblock From crowdsourced data to high-quality benchmarks: Arena-Hard and BenchBuilder pipeline.\n496\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2406.11939}, 2024.\n497\t\n498\t\\bibitem[Likert(1932)]{likert1932technique}\n499\tLikert, R.\n500\t\\newblock A technique for the measurement of attitudes.\n501\t\\newblock \\emph{Archives of Psychology}, 22(140):5--55, 1932.\n502\t\n503\t\\bibitem[Liu et~al.(2023)]{liu2023geval}\n504\tLiu, Y., Iter, D., Xu, Y., et~al.\n505\t\\newblock {G-Eval}: {NLG} evaluation using {GPT-4} with better human alignment.\n506\t\\newblock In \\emph{EMNLP}, 2023.\n507\t\n508\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2025)]{lu2025casse}\n509\tLu, K., et~al.\n510\t\\newblock {CASSE}: A comprehensive assessment of style and semantic effects in {LLM} judges.\n511\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2501.xxxxx}, 2025.\n512\t\n513\t\\bibitem[Mir et~al.(2019)]{mir2019evaluating}\n514\tMir, R., Felber, B., Obradovi\\'{c}, C., and Durrani, N.\n515\t\\newblock Evaluating style transfer for text.\n516\t\\newblock In \\emph{NAACL}, 2019.\n517\t\n518\t\\bibitem[Ouyang et~al.(2022)]{ouyang2022training}\n519\tOuyang, L., Wu, J., Jiang, X., et~al.\n520\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n521\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2022.\n522\t\n523\t\\bibitem[Park et~al.(2024)]{park2024disentangling}\n524\tPark, J., et~al.\n525\t\\newblock Disentangling length from quality in direct preference optimization.\n526\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n527\t\n528\t\\bibitem[Saito et~al.(2023)]{saito2023verbosity}\n529\tSaito, K., et~al.\n530\t\\newblock Verbosity bias in {LLM} evaluators.\n531\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2310.xxxxx}, 2023.\n532\t\n533\t\\bibitem[Thurstone(1927)]{thurstone1927law}\n534\tThurstone, L.~L.\n535\t\\newblock A law of comparative judgment.\n536\t\\newblock \\emph{Psychological Review}, 34(4):273--286, 1927.\n537\t\n538\t\\bibitem[Veitch et~al.(2020)]{veitch2020adapting}\n539\tVeitch, V., D'Amour, A., Yadlowsky, S., and Eisenstein, J.\n540\t\\newblock Adapting text embeddings for causal inference.\n541\t\\newblock In \\emph{UAI}, 2020.\n542\t\n543\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2023)]{wang2023chatgpt}\n544\tWang, J., Liang, Y., Meng, F., et~al.\n545\t\\newblock Is {ChatGPT} a good {NLG} evaluator? {A} preliminary study.\n546\t\\newblock In \\emph{NewSumm @ EMNLP}, 2023.\n547\t\n548\t\\bibitem[Wang et~al.(2024)]{wang2024faireval}\n549\tWang, P., Li, L., Chen, L., et~al.\n550\t\\newblock Large language models are not fair evaluators.\n551\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n552\t\n553\t\\bibitem[Wu et~al.(2024)]{wu2024style}\n554\tWu, M., Ahmadian, A., Duckworth, O., et~al.\n555\t\\newblock Style over substance: Evaluation biases for large language models.\n556\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n557\t\n558\t\\bibitem[Zeng et~al.(2024)]{zeng2024llmbar}\n559\tZeng, Z., Yu, J., Gao, T., et~al.\n560\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n561\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n562\t\n563\t\\bibitem[Zhang et~al.(2020)]{zhang2020bertscore}\n564\tZhang, T., Kishore, V., Wu, F., Weinberger, K.~Q., and Artzi, Y.\n565\t\\newblock {BERTScore}: Evaluating text generation with {BERT}.\n566\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2020.\n567\t\n568\t\\bibitem[Zheng et~al.(2023)]{zheng2023judging}\n569\tZheng, L., Chiang, W.-L., Sheng, Y., et~al.\n570\t\\newblock Judging {LLM}-as-a-judge with {MT}-Bench and Chatbot Arena.\n571\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n572\t\n573\t\\bibitem[Zhou et~al.(2025)]{zhou2025counterfactual}\n574\tZhou, Y., et~al.\n575\t\\newblock Counterfactual rhetorical evaluation of {LLM} judges.\n576\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2502.xxxxx}, 2025.\n577\t\n578\t\\end{thebibliography}\n579\t\n580\t% ============================================================\n581\t\\appendix\n582\t\\section{Additional Results}\n583\t\n584\t\\paragraph{Register axis details.}\n585\tOn LLMBar with $n$=100: GPT-4o academic win rate 66.0\\% (35 wins, 18 losses, $n_c$=53, $p$=0.015), Claude 62.0\\% (31/19, $n_c$=50, $p$=0.042), Gemini 71.4\\% (40/16, $n_c$=56, $p$=0.003). NLI pass rate 86\\%, mean NLI 0.893. All Likert $p$-values non-significant ($p$=0.289, 0.511, 0.167).\n586\t\n587\t\\paragraph{Tone axis failure details.}\n588\tPilot ($n$=20): NLI pass 65\\%, mean NLI 0.704, joint pass 45\\%. Assertive$\\to$hedging: both-pass 38.5\\%, 4/13 catastrophic NLI. Hedging$\\to$assertive: both-pass 57.1\\%, 2/7 catastrophic. The asymmetry (weakening assertions more often preserves entailment than strengthening them) is itself evidence that the NLI gate correctly identifies semantic change.\n589\t\n590\t\\paragraph{Rewriter ablation.}\n591\tGPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar formality: Qwen3-32B yields NLI pass 88\\%, formal WR 69.1\\% ($p$=0.006, $n_c$=55); Llama-3.1-70B yields NLI pass 85\\%, formal WR 66.0\\% ($p$=0.015, $n_c$=52). Fisher exact test on the win rate difference: $p$=0.42.\n592\t\n593\t\\paragraph{Consistency filter analysis.}\n594\tAcross 98 valid pairs (GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar): 55 consistent, 43 inconsistent. No significant differences in NLI mean (0.903 vs.\\ 0.886, $p$=0.42), text length (142.3 vs.\\ 137.8 words, $p$=0.58), BERTScore (0.848 vs.\\ 0.834, $p$=0.38), formal-original fraction (60.0\\% vs.\\ 55.8\\%, $\\chi^2$ $p$=0.67), or task type distribution ($p$=0.73).\n595\t\n596\t\\paragraph{Human NLI agreement.}\n597\tFrom 80 annotated pairs: 54 both-preserved, 14 both-not-preserved, 4 NLI false positives, 8 NLI false negatives. Raw agreement 85\\%, Cohen's $\\kappa$=0.72. The low false positive rate (6.9\\%) means pairs passing the NLI gate reliably preserve content.\n598\t\n599\t\\end{document}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SC45WTGFdy1q8mt8TkGri4","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"8ca060a4-e467-4d10-9b83-bae3fc06c2c1","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:32:26.891Z"}
