{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KWrJJoH8KtyfvVq8AsrMT7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260520T233719\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 3.5/6 — Borderline Conference / Strong Findings\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-21T00:28:14.344592+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t### R2 Review: Novelty + Related Work\n9\t\n10\t**Summary**\n11\t\n12\tThis paper combines LLM-based counterfactual style rewriting, NLI content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison to detect per-axis style bias in black-box LLM judges. The central finding — the \"Likert Blindspot\" — is that standard Likert pointwise scoring fails to detect formality bias (0/9 judge x benchmark combinations significant) while pairwise comparison on the same data detects it reliably (7/9 significant, mean win rate 66.4%). The binarized Likert control, register replication, and tone negative control provide additional evidence. The paper is honest about combining known components and positions itself as an empirical contribution.\n13\t\n14\t**Strengths**\n15\t\n16\t1. **The Likert Blindspot is the right primary contribution.** The 0/9 vs. 7/9 contrast is systematic and striking. The paper correctly identifies that nobody has performed this head-to-head comparison for style bias detection, and the finding has immediate practical implications for the large community using Likert-based LLM evaluation. The naming is effective — \"Likert Blindspot\" is memorable and citable.\n17\t\n18\t2. **The binarized Likert control (Table 3) is the paper's most novel methodological element.** By applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores, the paper isolates measurement granularity from test selection as the mechanism. This design eliminates the most damaging alternative explanation (that pairwise uses sign test while Likert uses t-test, and the gap is just a test artifact). This control, to my knowledge, has not appeared in the Likert-vs-pairwise NLG evaluation literature.\n19\t\n20\t3. **Honest contribution framing substantially improves over D1.** C1 is explicitly labeled \"application,\" C3 \"preliminary,\" component sources are acknowledged (RATE, Mir et al., Zheng et al.). This calibration is appropriate and builds reviewer trust. The title shift from \"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals...\" to \"The Likert Blindspot: Detecting...\" correctly de-emphasizes the method and foregrounds the finding.\n21\t\n22\t4. **The tone negative control is more informative than a third positive axis.** The mechanistic analysis (epistemic commitment changes violate NLI, 30% catastrophic failures, asymmetry between assertive-to-hedging vs. hedging-to-assertive) establishes a principled method boundary. This prevents the paper from overclaiming generalizability.\n23\t\n24\t5. **Comprehensive robustness battery.** NLI threshold sensitivity (Table 6), rewriter ablation (Qwen3 vs. Llama), n=500 replication (Table 5), consistency filter analysis, human NLI validation (kappa=0.72), original preference RATE control (Table 7), and logistic regression decomposition (Table 8). The density of robustness checks is above average for this venue.\n25\t\n26\t**Weaknesses**\n27\t\n28\t1. **(Major) The core finding confirms a known psychometric principle — novelty is empirical confirmation, not discovery.** Thurstone (1927) established that paired comparison is more sensitive than absolute scaling. Jamieson (2004) documented Likert scale insensitivity. Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017) demonstrated best-worst scaling outperforms Likert for NLG evaluation. Karpinska et al. (2021), cited in the paper, documented Likert reliability problems for text quality assessment. The mechanism identified (measurement granularity, tied scores, scale resolution) was predictable from this prior work. The paper's contribution is quantifying the gap magnitude in the specific context of LLM judge style bias — this is useful but should be framed as \"empirical demonstration of a predicted effect in a high-stakes domain\" rather than as a finding that \"challenges conventional wisdom.\" The abstract and introduction currently read as if this is surprising; the psychometrics community would not be surprised. The paper should more explicitly connect to this lineage and position the novelty as: (a) the first quantification of the gap magnitude for style bias specifically, and (b) the binarized Likert control design that isolates the mechanism.\n29\t\n30\t2. **(Major) Insufficient differentiation from RATE (Feuer et al. 2025) and Zhou et al. (2025).** RATE provides the double-rewrite ATE protocol that CSD's original preference control directly adopts. Zhou et al. (2025) apply persona-level counterfactuals with Bradley-Terry aggregation to evaluate LLM judges — the closest methodological predecessor. Yet RATE gets one sentence in the counterfactual evaluation paragraph and Zhou et al. gets one sentence. A reader needs to understand: what can CSD detect that RATE cannot? What does CSD's per-axis approach give you that Zhou et al.'s persona-level approach does not? The paper should include either a comparison table or a dedicated paragraph articulating these deltas. Specifically: (a) RATE measures overall rewrite quality effects, CSD targets isolated style dimensions; (b) RATE uses Likert ATE (which this paper shows is insensitive!), CSD uses pairwise; (c) Zhou et al. operate at persona granularity, CSD at style-axis granularity. These are genuine differentiators but they are currently implicit.\n31\t\n32\t3. **(Major) \"Causal\" language exceeds the formal identification support.** The method name \"Counterfactual Style Decomposition\" and phrases like \"independent causal effect\" (Section 5.5), \"causal factor\" (Conclusion) imply formal causal identification. The paper provides no DAG, no SUTVA discussion, no sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding. The logistic regression (Table 8) is associational with measured confounders — it does not constitute causal identification. The counterfactual rewriting + NLI verification provides strong *interventional* evidence, but formal causal claims require stating identifying assumptions explicitly. The paper should either: (a) provide a minimal DAG with stated assumptions (e.g., no unmeasured common causes of style and judge preference beyond those controlled), or (b) soften language throughout to \"interventional\" / \"quasi-experimental\" / \"manipulated.\" I recommend (b) as the lower-risk path that loses nothing substantive.\n33\t\n34\t4. **(Major) Binarized Likert control shown for only 1/9 combinations.** Table 3 presents the decisive C2 evidence but only for GPT-4o x LLMBar. This is the paper's single most important result — it should be replicated across at minimum the 3 LLMBar combinations. Gemini x LLMBar has the strongest pairwise effect (72.4%); if binarized Likert reaches significance there, the \"structural measurement limitation\" narrative weakens to \"scale-and-effect-size-dependent limitation.\" This is a zero-cost reanalysis of existing Likert data. Its absence for 8/9 combinations is conspicuous.\n35\t\n36\t5. **(Minor) Several related work gaps.** The paper cites ~28 references, adequate for the venue but missing several relevant works:\n37\t   - **Stureborg et al. (2024), \"Large Language Models are Inconsistent and Biased Evaluators\"**: Directly relevant to measurement methodology for LLM judge bias.\n38\t   - **Ye et al. (2024), FLASK**: Multi-dimensional LLM evaluation with fine-grained scoring — relevant comparison for per-axis assessment.\n39\t   - **Li et al. (2024), \"Split and Merge\"**: Position bias mitigation strategy that complements the paper's position-control approach.\n40\t   - **Preference Leakage (Li et al., ICLR 2026)**: Listed in the project's own literature map as a foundation paper documenting style as a preference leakage carrier. Not cited in the paper. Should be discussed as it validates the premise from a different angle (style bias propagates through the RLHF pipeline).\n41\t   - **Soumik et al. (2026), \"Judging the Judges\"**: Also in the literature map, reports bias scores 0.76-0.92. Chen et al. [2026] appears in the bibliography but with a placeholder arXiv ID (2601.xxxxx) — unclear if this is the same paper or a different one.\n42\t\n43\t6. **(Minor) Placeholder arXiv IDs for 4 references.** Chen et al. [2026] (2601.xxxxx), Lu et al. [2025] CASSE (2501.xxxxx), Saito et al. [2023] (2310.xxxxx), Zhou et al. [2025] (2502.xxxxx). If these are real papers, actual IDs must be provided. If any are fabricated, this is a serious integrity issue. Currently impossible to verify.\n44\t\n45\t7. **(Minor) SOS-Bench positioning is slightly unfair.** The paper characterizes SOS-Bench as providing \"correlational\" evidence. While SOS-Bench does not perform the same kind of counterfactual isolation, its controlled defect injection methodology (inserting known errors, removing style features) across 152K data points is itself interventional. The paper should acknowledge SOS-Bench's intervention design while noting the difference: SOS-Bench injects quality defects to measure judge sensitivity, CSD manipulates style to measure style-specific bias. These are complementary, not hierarchically ordered.\n46\t\n47\t8. **(Minor) The CSD-adjusted win rate (C4) is thin.** The correction amounts to subtracting a 3.3-point original preference estimate from the raw win rate. Compared to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework — which provides a full debiased leaderboard, generalized linear model, and is deployed in production — C4 is four bullet points of practitioner guidance plus a rudimentary correction. This is acknowledged in limitations but the contribution list elevates it to a numbered claim (C4), which invites comparison with more mature correction methods.\n48\t\n49\t**Questions for Authors**\n50\t\n51\t1. What does the binarized Likert control look like for Gemini x LLMBar (strongest effect, 72.4%)? If binarized Likert is significant there, does C2 become \"the Likert Blindspot exists for moderate effects but not large ones\" — and how does that affect the practical implications?\n52\t\n53\t2. Can you provide verified arXiv IDs for all four placeholder references? Are Chen et al. [2026] and Soumik et al. (2026) the same paper?\n54\t\n55\t3. The logistic regression (Table 8) pools N=459 observations from pairs evaluated in both directions. Did you account for within-pair clustering (e.g., GEE, mixed-effects)? The decisions.yaml mentions a GEE fix was applied (D018) — the paper should report both naive and clustered results for transparency.\n56\t\n57\t4. RATE uses Likert-based ATE as its measurement. Your paper shows Likert ATE is insensitive to style effects. Does this mean RATE's own style-related findings may also suffer from the Likert Blindspot? This would be a significant implication worth discussing — it would position CSD as not just an alternative but a correction to RATE's measurement methodology.\n58\t\n59\t5. The paper claims the judge gradient (Gemini > GPT-4o > Claude) \"likely reflects differences in RLHF calibration — lighter models rely more on surface heuristics.\" This is speculative and potentially misleading (Gemini-Flash is not straightforwardly \"lighter\" than Claude-Sonnet). Can you provide evidence for this claim or soften it?\n60\t\n61\t6. Would the Likert Blindspot persist with continuous scales (1-100, slider) or with judge log-probabilities? Without this, the generalizability of C2 is limited to discrete Likert scales specifically, which should be stated explicitly as a scope limitation.\n62\t\n63\t**Novelty Assessment**\n64\t\n65\tThe paper's novelty sits at the intersection of \"known principle, new domain, new quantification.\" Each individual component — counterfactual rewriting, NLI verification, pairwise comparison, the psychometric superiority of forced choice over absolute scaling — is established. The combination is natural rather than surprising. The paper is honest about this.\n66\t\n67\tWhat IS genuinely new: (1) the binarized Likert control design, which I have not seen in prior work and which provides a clean mechanism isolation; (2) the specific quantification that the Likert Blindspot produces a 30x p-value gap (median 0.39 vs. 0.012) with zero reverse cases across 9 combinations; (3) the tone negative control as a principled method boundary with mechanistic explanation.\n68\t\n69\tWhat is NOT new: the finding that pairwise is more sensitive than Likert (Thurstone 1927, Kiritchenko and Mohammad 2017); the counterfactual rewriting pipeline (RATE, Zhou et al.); applying counterfactuals to LLM judge evaluation (Zhou et al. 2025).\n70\t\n71\tFor EMNLP, the novelty bar requires either a new method or a new finding that changes practice. This paper offers the latter: a specific, well-quantified demonstration that the dominant evaluation paradigm (Likert scoring) has a systematic blind spot for style bias, with a simple diagnostic (pairwise comparison on counterfactual pairs) that practitioners can adopt immediately. The binarized Likert control adds genuine methodological novelty. This is sufficient for the venue, provided the framing remains calibrated (application + empirical contribution, not framework).\n72\t\n73\t**Related Work Gaps**\n74\t\n75\tThe related work is adequate in breadth (4 paragraphs, covering LLM-as-judge, style bias, counterfactual methods, measurement methodology) but thin in depth on the two closest competitors:\n76\t\n77\t- **RATE**: Needs a paragraph, not a sentence. The delta (per-axis targeting, pairwise instead of Likert ATE, NLI verification gate) should be spelled out. The ironic implication — that RATE's own Likert-based measurement may suffer from the blindspot this paper identifies — is unexploited.\n78\t- **Zhou et al. (2025) Counterfactual Rhetorical**: Needs explicit comparison of granularity (persona vs. style axis), aggregation method (Bradley-Terry vs. sign test), and scope (rhetorical style vs. surface style features).\n79\t- **Preference Leakage (ICLR 2026)**: Should be cited as establishing the downstream impact chain (style bias in judges propagates to reward models to policies), which strengthens the motivation.\n80\t- **Novikova et al. (2018)**: An important reference on Likert vs. ranking for NLG evaluation that appears in the paper's own framing documents but not in the bibliography.\n81\t\n82\tThe measurement methodology paragraph (currently the shortest) should be expanded with the Kiritchenko and Mohammad (2017) and Novikova et al. (2018) results, explicitly noting that the pairwise advantage is predicted by this literature and that the paper's contribution is quantifying it for LLM judge style bias specifically.\n83\t\n84\t**Significance / Impact Assessment**\n85\t\n86\tThe paper's practical impact depends on whether the community adopts pairwise comparison for style bias auditing. The finding is actionable — a practitioner can implement the counterfactual-pairwise pipeline with existing tools (LLM rewriter, off-the-shelf NLI model, any judge API). The recommendation to prefer pairwise over Likert for bias auditing is concrete and low-cost to follow.\n87\t\n88\tHowever, several factors limit impact: (1) the correction mechanism (C4) is rudimentary compared to LC-AlpacaEval; (2) only two working style axes (formality, register) are demonstrated, with register shown only on one benchmark; (3) the finding does not change how people BUILD judges, only how they AUDIT them; (4) the n=100 per cell and 44% discard rate from consistency filtering mean the pipeline requires substantial API budget for production use.\n89\t\n90\tThe Likert Blindspot finding, if confirmed with real data across the full 3x3 matrix, would be a meaningful contribution to the LLM evaluation methodology literature. It would not reshape the field but would provide a useful diagnostic tool and a concrete recommendation for benchmark designers.\n91\t\n92\t**Score:** 3.5/6\n93\t\n94\t**Confidence:** high\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t**Score Justification:** The paper sits between Findings (3.0) and Weak Accept (4.0). The Likert Blindspot is a real and useful finding, the binarized Likert control is clever, and the robustness battery is thorough. However: (1) the core finding confirms a predicted psychometric effect rather than discovering a new one — novelty is empirical quantification in a new domain; (2) differentiation from RATE and Zhou et al. is underdeveloped; (3) causal language overclaims; (4) the binarized Likert control appears for only 1/9 combinations; (5) the correction mechanism is thin. With the identified revisions (extend binarized Likert to all 9 cells, soften causal language, strengthen RATE/Zhou differentiation, add missing references), the paper could reach 4.0. As submitted, it is a strong Findings paper that falls just short of the Main conference threshold on novelty grounds. The 3.5 reflects \"borderline conference / strong Findings\" per the EMNLP 2026 scale."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SC45WTGFdy1q8mt8TkGri4","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"3a5dc94a-dd42-4341-973c-01112201f3f8","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:32:32.140Z"}
