{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VC7YZzXy4NwrrUm73VuQr6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EErn9BxGUWJRaE3ciMRic2","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T233719/worst/paper.tex","limit":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3183,"cache_read_input_tokens":60589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3183,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"8e0f71c7-9159-46f7-b4e0-d23a41870588"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EErn9BxGUWJRaE3ciMRic2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n14\t\\usepackage{array}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\etal}{\\textit{et al.}}\n17\t\\newcommand{\\ie}{\\textit{i.e.}}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\eg}{\\textit{e.g.}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\cf}{\\textit{cf.}}\n20\t\n21\t\\title{Formality Bias or Original-Text Preference?\\\\A Cautionary Analysis of Confounds\\\\in LLM Judge Style Bias Detection}\n22\t\n23\t\\author{Anonymous}\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t% ABSTRACT\n31\t% ============================================================\n32\t\\begin{abstract}\n33\tLLM-as-judge systems are increasingly used for evaluation and alignment, yet their susceptibility to surface-level style biases remains poorly understood. We investigate formality bias across three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench) using counterfactual style interventions with NLI-based content verification. Our initial finding---that pairwise comparison detects significant formality preference where Likert scoring does not---does not survive deeper scrutiny. A binarized Likert sign test recovers the signal ($p=0.032$), showing the Likert--pairwise gap reflects statistical test choice, not measurement structure. More critically, logistic regression reveals that \\emph{original-text preference} ($\\beta=0.65$, $p=0.002$) rather than formality ($\\beta=0.28$, $p=0.21$) drives observed effects: when the formal version is a rewrite rather than the original, formal win rate drops to 52.6\\% ($p=0.72$). The effect is further confined to a single judge--benchmark combination (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar); neither Claude nor Gemini shows significant formality preference on any benchmark. We release our analysis as a cautionary case study demonstrating that counterfactual bias detection methods must control for original-text preference, and provide practical recommendations for researchers conducting LLM judge bias audits.\n34\t\\end{abstract}\n35\t\n36\t% ============================================================\n37\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n38\t% ============================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become critical infrastructure in NLP, from leaderboard ranking \\citep{zheng2024chatbot,li2024alpacaeval} to RLHF reward signal generation. Recent work reveals that LLM judges exhibit systematic surface-level biases: length bias inflates scores for verbose responses \\citep{dubois2024alpacaeval}, and broader style biases dominate quality signals with correlations as high as $R=0.999$ \\citep{sosbench2025}.\n42\t\n43\tA natural approach to studying such biases is counterfactual intervention: rewrite a response to change only its style, then measure whether the judge's preference shifts. This approach, grounded in the potential outcomes framework \\citep{rubin2005causal}, has been applied to length \\citep{dubois2024alpacaeval}, rhetorical style \\citep{counterfactual_rhetorical2025}, and multi-dimensional style variation \\citep{casse2026}. The RATE protocol \\citep{rate2025} provides a principled double-rewrite design for estimating average treatment effects.\n44\t\n45\tWe apply this methodology to formality bias, pairing counterfactual style transfer with NLI-based content verification and position-controlled pairwise comparison. Our initial results appear promising: on LLMBar with GPT-4o as judge, formal responses win 69.1\\% of pairwise comparisons ($p=0.006$) while Likert scoring shows no significant effect ($p=0.332$). This ``Likert Blindspot'' seems to reveal a measurement gap with broad implications.\n46\t\n47\tHowever, systematic investigation reveals this finding does not generalize and is confounded:\n48\t\n49\t\\begin{enumerate}\n50\t    \\item \\textbf{Judge-specific, not universal.} Claude-3.5-Sonnet (54.5\\%, $p=0.42$) and Gemini-2.0-Flash (52.1\\%, $p=0.68$) show no formality preference. Across 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, only 1 reaches significance before correction; none survive Bonferroni adjustment.\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{The Likert gap is statistical, not structural.} A binarized Likert sign test---using the same Likert data but testing directional preference rather than mean difference---is significant ($p=0.032$). The gap arises from paired $t$-test sensitivity to score variance on coarse 1--5 scales, not from a structural advantage of pairwise comparison.\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Original-text preference is the primary confound.} Logistic regression shows original-text status ($p=0.002$) but not formality ($p=0.21$) predicts judge preference. In the casual-original subset where formality and original status conflict, formal win rate is 52.6\\% ($p=0.72$)---indistinguishable from chance.\n53\t\\end{enumerate}\n54\t\n55\tThese negative results carry a constructive message. We demonstrate that counterfactual bias detection inherently confounds style preference with original-text preference whenever one direction is the unmodified original. We formalize this confound via logistic decomposition and show it explains the entirety of observed ``formality bias'' in our experiments. Our practical recommendations---always control for original status, report directional subset analyses, use sign tests over $t$-tests on ordinal scales---aim to prevent future studies from drawing incorrect conclusions about LLM judge bias.\n56\t\n57\t% ============================================================\n58\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n59\t% ============================================================\n60\t\\section{Related Work}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"5058dc65-cd61-4fc7-bdf8-cb9c5f2e74d3","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:32:35.592Z"}
