{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VC7YZzXy4NwrrUm73VuQr6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XAGx1qknTLFEjVpiB8T2VD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T233719/median/paper.tex","limit":60},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3183,"cache_read_input_tokens":60589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3183,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"c91750d3-b5df-4f71-8029-f93ae48c287f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XAGx1qknTLFEjVpiB8T2VD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{geometry}\n6\t\\geometry{margin=1in}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n10\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\usepackage{caption}\n15\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n16\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n17\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n18\t\n19\t\\newcommand{\\system}{CSD}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\tmark}[1]{\\textsuperscript{#1}}\n21\t\n22\t\\title{The Likert Blindspot: Detecting Hidden Style Bias in LLM Judges\\\\via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n23\t\n24\t\\author{Anonymous}\n25\t\\date{}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t% ABSTRACT\n32\t% ============================================================\n33\t\\begin{abstract}\n34\tLLM-as-judge systems are increasingly central to NLP evaluation and alignment training, yet their susceptibility to surface-level style bias remains poorly characterized under standard evaluation protocols.\n35\tWe apply counterfactual style decomposition---combining LLM-based style rewriting, NLI-based content verification, and position-controlled pairwise comparison---to measure per-axis causal style bias in black-box LLM judges.\n36\tOn the formality axis, GPT-4o exhibits a significant preference for formal text in pairwise comparison (69.1\\% win rate, $p=0.006$, $n=55$), replicated on Claude-3.5-Sonnet ($64.7\\%$, $p=0.037$) though not on Gemini-2.0-Flash ($56.3\\%$, $p=0.44$).\n37\tCritically, standard Likert pointwise scoring on the same counterfactual data detects no significant bias (ATE$=0.10$, $p=0.33$), revealing a \\emph{Likert blindspot} whereby the dominant evaluation paradigm systematically underestimates style bias---though the source of this sensitivity gap (measurement structure vs.\\ statistical power) remains partially ambiguous.\n38\tScaled replication ($n=500$) confirms the aggregate effect ($p<0.001$) but also reveals that observed formality preference partly reflects original-text preference correlated with formality, a confound we characterize via logistic regression.\n39\tWe provide axis-applicability analysis showing that content-preserving counterfactual intervention is feasible for formality but structurally incompatible with axes that alter epistemic commitment (tone) or information quantity (verbosity).\n40\tOur findings suggest that practitioners should prefer pairwise protocols for style bias auditing and control for original-text preference when interpreting results.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t% ============================================================\n44\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n45\t% ============================================================\n46\t\\section{Introduction}\n47\t\n48\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become critical infrastructure for NLP benchmarking~\\citep{zheng2023judging,dubois2024alpacafarm} and reinforcement learning from AI feedback~\\citep{bai2022constitutional,lee2023rlaif}.\n49\tRecent work documents strong style biases in these systems: length bias dominates Chatbot Arena~\\citep{park2024disentangling}, and SOS-Bench reports near-perfect correlation ($R=0.999$) between judge scores and superficial style features~\\citep{sosbench2025}.\n50\tYet the standard Likert pointwise scoring protocol used by most benchmarks may itself be masking the problem.\n51\t\n52\tWe find that standard Likert scoring declares no style bias ($p=0.33$), while position-controlled pairwise comparison on \\emph{the same counterfactual data} reveals significant formality preference ($69.1\\%$, $p=0.006$)---a Likert blindspot that persists across judges and benchmarks in our experiments.\n53\tThis is not merely an academic curiosity: if the dominant evaluation paradigm systematically underestimates style bias, then debiasing efforts calibrated on Likert-based audits will be insufficient.\n54\t\n55\tOur approach combines three known components in a new application: (1) LLM-based counterfactual style rewriting to generate matched pairs differing only in a target style axis, (2) NLI-based content verification to ensure semantic preservation, and (3) position-controlled pairwise comparison to measure causal style preference.\n56\tWe explicitly acknowledge that none of these components is novel in isolation---counterfactual rewriting follows RATE~\\citep{feuer2025rate}, NLI verification draws from style transfer evaluation~\\citep{mir2019evaluating}, and pairwise comparison is standard~\\citep{zheng2023judging}.\n57\tThe contribution is empirical: demonstrating that their combination reveals bias invisible to Likert scoring, and characterizing the conditions under which this approach succeeds or fails.\n58\t\n59\tOur key findings:\n60\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"2da8f1e1-0a2a-42b8-8a31-8488c60e8dd6","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:32:36.036Z"}
