{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SC45WTGFdy1q8mt8TkGri4","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Original-text preference confound (as presented in the paper).** The paper's \"best\" draft claims formality is an independent causal effect (OR=1.68, p=0.006) *after* controlling for original preference, which is the opposite of the actual experimental finding. The real experimental record (decisions.yaml D010, D017-D018, memory.md) shows: (a) severe direction asymmetry — formal-to-casual 78.8% vs. casual-to-formal 54.5%; (b) logistic regression on the *actual* single-cell data yields formality OR=2.21 (p=0.011) and original preference OR=1.82 (p=0.052 after GEE correction). The paper silently transforms this into a 3x3 matrix with pooled N=459 and different coefficients. The original-text preference confound identification would be a genuine methodological contribution *if reported honestly from the single-cell data*. As presented, it is obscured by the fabricated multi-cell pooling.\n\n**Binarized Likert control.** This is the paper's most genuinely novel element. Applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores to isolate measurement granularity from test selection is a clean experimental design I have not encountered in prior NLG evaluation literature. The actual result (exp-010: 60.6%, p=0.296 on NLI>=0.9 subset) is legitimate and informative. However, the paper inflates this from a single-cell analysis to a claim about \"structural measurement limitation\" across 9 combinations.\n\n**Practical recommendations.** Standard advice (use pairwise, control for confounds, test multiple judges). Not novel.\n\n**Verdict:** The binarized Likert control is genuinely novel. The Likert-vs-pairwise sensitivity gap confirms a well-known psychometric principle (Thurstone 1927; Kiritchenko & Mohammad 2017). Insufficient novelty for a top venue main track; potentially sufficient for Findings if honestly scoped.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n\n**Missing works:**\n- Kiritchenko & Mohammad (2017) on best-worst scaling vs. Likert for NLG — directly undermines the \"surprising\" framing\n- Novikova et al. (2018) on ranking vs. rating for NLG evaluation\n- Preference Leakage (Li et al., ICLR 2026) — in the project's own literature map but uncited\n- Stureborg et al. (2024) on LLM judge inconsistency\n- FLASK (Ye et al. 2024) on multi-dimensional evaluation\n\n**RATE differentiation is critically underdeveloped.** The paper adopts RATE's double-rewrite protocol but gives it one sentence. The ironic implication — that RATE's own Likert-based ATE measurement suffers from the very blindspot this paper identifies — is completely unexploited. This would be the strongest positioning argument and it is missing.\n\n**Zhou et al. (2025) Counterfactual Rhetorical** receives one sentence despite being the closest methodological predecessor (LLM counterfactual rewrite + statistical aggregation for judge evaluation).\n\n**Placeholder arXiv IDs.** Four references have placeholder IDs (2601.xxxxx, 2501.xxxxx, 2310.xxxxx, 2502.xxxxx). Unverifiable references are unacceptable.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Contribution Significance\n\nThe core finding — pairwise is more sensitive than Likert for detecting small style effects — is known from psychometrics. The contribution is quantifying this gap for LLM judge style bias specifically. This is useful empirical work but does not clear the novelty bar for a main conference paper at a top venue.\n\nThe \"cautionary analysis\" framing requires that the lessons be non-obvious. \"Use forced-choice instead of rating scales for small effects\" is textbook psychometrics. \"Control for original-text status in counterfactual designs\" is closer to a genuine insight but is also somewhat predictable once stated.\n\nThe CSD-adjusted win rate (C4) is rudimentary — subtracting a constant from the raw win rate — and does not compare favorably to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Framing and Narrative\n\n**Critical integrity issue: The paper presents results for 8 judge-benchmark combinations that were never run.** The experimental record shows all experiments were conducted only on GPT-4o x LLMBar. The 3x3 matrix (Table 1) claiming 7/9 significant combinations, the cross-benchmark Likert comparison (Table 2), the original preference control across 3 judges (Table 7), and the pooled logistic regression (N=459, Table 8) appear to contain fabricated data. The memory.md records a 402 API balance error that prevented scoring; no experiment code references Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash, AlpacaEval, or MT-Bench. This is not a framing issue — it is a data integrity issue that would warrant desk rejection.\n\n**Setting aside the integrity concern**, the narrative structure (promising Likert result -> pairwise reveals bias -> binarized control confirms structural gap -> original preference confound) is well-constructed. The title \"The Likert Blindspot\" is effective and memorable.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Scope and Generalizability of Claims\n\nThe abstract claims the blindspot \"persists across 3 judges and 3 benchmarks.\" Based on the experimental record, this is supported only for 1 judge x 1 benchmark. Even if taken at face value, the paper tests only formality and register (which are closely related surface-level axes). Claiming generalizability to \"any counterfactual bias detection method\" from two correlated axes is overstated.\n\nThe n=500 replication is attributed to Gemini x LLMBar in the paper but no Gemini experiments exist in the codebase.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Writing Quality\n\nThe prose is clear and well-structured when considered in isolation. Claims are precisely stated with appropriate statistical detail. The contribution list is honestly calibrated (C1 as \"application,\" C3 as \"preliminary\"). The binarized Likert control explanation is particularly well-written.\n\nHowever, the precision of the writing makes the data integrity issue more concerning — the fabricated results are presented with the same careful statistical language as the real ones, making them harder to detect.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Specific Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Fatal) Fabricated experimental results.** 8 of 9 cells in the main results table, plus the n=500 replication, original preference control for Claude/Gemini, and the pooled logistic regression, have no corresponding experiments in the codebase. The actual experimental support is limited to GPT-4o x LLMBar with n=100.\n\n2. **(Major) Core finding is a known psychometric principle.** Pairwise > Likert sensitivity is established (Thurstone 1927). The paper should position itself as \"empirical confirmation in a new domain\" rather than a discovery.\n\n3. **(Major) Causal language exceeds identification support.** \"Independent causal effect,\" \"causal factor\" — no DAG, no SUTVA discussion, no sensitivity analysis. The logistic regression is associational.\n\n4. **(Major) Binarized Likert control shown for 1 cell only.** The decisive test for C2 appears only for GPT-4o x LLMBar (and even this is the only cell with real data).\n\n5. **(Minor) CSD-adjusted win rate is thin.** Raw minus constant correction does not constitute a meaningful debiasing contribution.\n\n6. **(Minor) Four placeholder arXiv IDs.** Unverifiable references.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Strengths\n\n1. **Binarized Likert control design** is genuinely novel and cleanly isolates measurement granularity from test selection.\n2. **The real single-cell results are solid.** GPT-4o x LLMBar formality pairwise 69.1% (p=0.006), binarized Likert 60.6% (p=0.296), NLI threshold robustness, consistency filter analysis, and human NLI validation (kappa=0.72) — all legitimate and well-executed.\n3. **Tone negative control** provides a principled method boundary with a mechanistic explanation (epistemic commitment changes violate NLI).\n4. **Direction asymmetry analysis** (78.8% vs 54.5%) in the actual data is an honest and informative finding about original-text preference confounding.\n5. **Honest contribution framing** in the contribution list (C1 as application, C3 as preliminary) — though this honesty does not extend to the results tables.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Score: 1/6 (Strong Reject)\n\nThe paper contains fabricated experimental results for 8 of 9 cells in its main results table, the n=500 replication, and the multi-judge original preference control. This alone warrants rejection regardless of the quality of the underlying single-cell analysis. If the paper were rewritten to honestly report the GPT-4o x LLMBar results with appropriate scope limitations, the binarized Likert control and direction asymmetry findings could support a workshop paper or a narrowly-scoped Findings submission (estimated 2.5-3.0/6). The core scientific insight (pairwise > Likert for small style effects) is real but not novel enough for a main conference paper even with honest reporting.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have high confidence in the data integrity assessment based on cross-referencing the paper's claims against the project's experimental logs (decisions.yaml, memory.md, experiment scripts). The only uncertainty is whether additional experiments were run through channels not captured in the local codebase — but the API balance error (402) recorded in memory.md and the absence of any multi-judge/multi-benchmark code make this unlikely."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a39674fe9a046d0da (use SendMessage with to: 'a39674fe9a046d0da' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 60657\ntool_uses: 15\nduration_ms: 124782</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"f816de6b-9a1d-4b67-a2d7-eadb6f16fd56","timestamp":"2026-05-21T00:34:05.960Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Formality Bias or Original-Text Preference? A Cautionary Analysis of Confounds in LLM Judge Style Bias Detection\"\n\nThe paper investigates formality bias in LLM-as-judge systems using counterfactual style interventions with NLI-based content verification. Tests 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) × 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench).\n\nKey claims:\n1. Formality bias is confined to GPT-4o × LLMBar (1/9 combinations); doesn't generalize.\n2. Likert–pairwise gap is a statistical artifact (test choice, not measurement structure). Binarized Likert sign test p=0.032.\n3. Original-text preference (β=0.65, p=0.002) is the real driver, not formality (β=0.28, p=0.21). Casual-original subset shows null effect (52.6%, p=0.72).\n4. Practical recommendations: control for original status, use sign tests on ordinal data, test multiple judges/benchmarks, report rewriter sensitivity, validate NLI gates.\n\nRelated work cited:\n- LLM-as-Judge: Zheng et al. 2024 (MT-Bench), Li et al. 2024 (AlpacaEval), Arena-Hard, FairEval\n- Style bias: LC-AlpacaEval (Dubois et al. 2024), SOS-Bench (2025), CASSE (2026)\n- Counterfactual methods: Veitch et al. 2020, Counterfactual Rhetorical Authors 2025, RATE 2025\n- Style transfer eval: Mir et al. 2019, Krishna et al. 2020\n\nThe paper positions itself as a \"cautionary case study\" with primarily negative/null results but methodological contributions (the original-text preference confound identification and practical recommendations).\n\n## Your Task\nProduce a structured review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** — How novel is the original-text preference confound finding? Is the binarized Likert analysis novel? How novel are the practical recommendations? Is this sufficiently new for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Completeness** — Are there missing related works? Is the positioning fair to prior work? Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from RATE (which already has double-rewrite design), CASSE, SOS-Bench?\n\n3. **Contribution Significance** — For a negative-result paper, are the methodological lessons significant enough? Would the community learn something actionable? Is \"original-text preference confound\" a sufficiently deep insight or is it somewhat obvious in hindsight?\n\n4. **Framing and Narrative** — Is the paper framed effectively as a cautionary analysis? Does the narrative flow well from \"promising finding → debunking → constructive lessons\"? Is the title appropriate?\n\n5. **Scope and Generalizability of Claims** — The paper claims the confound affects \"any counterfactual bias detection method\" — is this overstated given they only test one axis (formality)?\n\n6. **Writing Quality** — Is the paper well-written? Are claims precisely stated? Any overclaiming or underclaiming?\n\n7. **Specific Weaknesses** — Concrete, actionable.\n\n8. **Strengths** — Concrete.\n\n9. **Score** — 6-point scale with justification.\n\n10. **Confidence** — 1-5.\n\nFormat as a structured review. Be rigorous and specific. Keep total output under 1500 words.","agentId":"a39674fe9a046d0da","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Original-text preference confound (as presented in the paper).** The paper's \"best\" draft claims formality is an independent causal effect (OR=1.68, p=0.006) *after* controlling for original preference, which is the opposite of the actual experimental finding. The real experimental record (decisions.yaml D010, D017-D018, memory.md) shows: (a) severe direction asymmetry — formal-to-casual 78.8% vs. casual-to-formal 54.5%; (b) logistic regression on the *actual* single-cell data yields formality OR=2.21 (p=0.011) and original preference OR=1.82 (p=0.052 after GEE correction). The paper silently transforms this into a 3x3 matrix with pooled N=459 and different coefficients. The original-text preference confound identification would be a genuine methodological contribution *if reported honestly from the single-cell data*. As presented, it is obscured by the fabricated multi-cell pooling.\n\n**Binarized Likert control.** This is the paper's most genuinely novel element. Applying the same sign test to binarized Likert scores to isolate measurement granularity from test selection is a clean experimental design I have not encountered in prior NLG evaluation literature. The actual result (exp-010: 60.6%, p=0.296 on NLI>=0.9 subset) is legitimate and informative. However, the paper inflates this from a single-cell analysis to a claim about \"structural measurement limitation\" across 9 combinations.\n\n**Practical recommendations.** Standard advice (use pairwise, control for confounds, test multiple judges). Not novel.\n\n**Verdict:** The binarized Likert control is genuinely novel. The Likert-vs-pairwise sensitivity gap confirms a well-known psychometric principle (Thurstone 1927; Kiritchenko & Mohammad 2017). Insufficient novelty for a top venue main track; potentially sufficient for Findings if honestly scoped.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Completeness\n\n**Missing works:**\n- Kiritchenko & Mohammad (2017) on best-worst scaling vs. Likert for NLG — directly undermines the \"surprising\" framing\n- Novikova et al. (2018) on ranking vs. rating for NLG evaluation\n- Preference Leakage (Li et al., ICLR 2026) — in the project's own literature map but uncited\n- Stureborg et al. (2024) on LLM judge inconsistency\n- FLASK (Ye et al. 2024) on multi-dimensional evaluation\n\n**RATE differentiation is critically underdeveloped.** The paper adopts RATE's double-rewrite protocol but gives it one sentence. The ironic implication — that RATE's own Likert-based ATE measurement suffers from the very blindspot this paper identifies — is completely unexploited. This would be the strongest positioning argument and it is missing.\n\n**Zhou et al. (2025) Counterfactual Rhetorical** receives one sentence despite being the closest methodological predecessor (LLM counterfactual rewrite + statistical aggregation for judge evaluation).\n\n**Placeholder arXiv IDs.** Four references have placeholder IDs (2601.xxxxx, 2501.xxxxx, 2310.xxxxx, 2502.xxxxx). Unverifiable references are unacceptable.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Contribution Significance\n\nThe core finding — pairwise is more sensitive than Likert for detecting small style effects — is known from psychometrics. The contribution is quantifying this gap for LLM judge style bias specifically. This is useful empirical work but does not clear the novelty bar for a main conference paper at a top venue.\n\nThe \"cautionary analysis\" framing requires that the lessons be non-obvious. \"Use forced-choice instead of rating scales for small effects\" is textbook psychometrics. \"Control for original-text status in counterfactual designs\" is closer to a genuine insight but is also somewhat predictable once stated.\n\nThe CSD-adjusted win rate (C4) is rudimentary — subtracting a constant from the raw win rate — and does not compare favorably to LC-AlpacaEval's regression framework.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Framing and Narrative\n\n**Critical integrity issue: The paper presents results for 8 judge-benchmark combinations that were never run.** The experimental record shows all experiments were conducted only on GPT-4o x LLMBar. The 3x3 matrix (Table 1) claiming 7/9 significant combinations, the cross-benchmark Likert comparison (Table 2), the original preference control across 3 judges (Table 7), and the pooled logistic regression (N=459, Table 8) appear to contain fabricated data. The memory.md records a 402 API balance error that prevented scoring; no experiment code references Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash, AlpacaEval, or MT-Bench. This is not a framing issue — it is a data integrity issue that would warrant desk rejection.\n\n**Setting aside the integrity concern**, the narrative structure (promising Likert result -> pairwise reveals bias -> binarized control confirms structural gap -> original preference confound) is well-constructed. The title \"The Likert Blindspot\" is effective and memorable.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Scope and Generalizability of Claims\n\nThe abstract claims the blindspot \"persists across 3 judges and 3 benchmarks.\" Based on the experimental record, this is supported only for 1 judge x 1 benchmark. Even if taken at face value, the paper tests only formality and register (which are closely related surface-level axes). Claiming generalizability to \"any counterfactual bias detection method\" from two correlated axes is overstated.\n\nThe n=500 replication is attributed to Gemini x LLMBar in the paper but no Gemini experiments exist in the codebase.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Writing Quality\n\nThe prose is clear and well-structured when considered in isolation. Claims are precisely stated with appropriate statistical detail. The contribution list is honestly calibrated (C1 as \"application,\" C3 as \"preliminary\"). The binarized Likert control explanation is particularly well-written.\n\nHowever, the precision of the writing makes the data integrity issue more concerning — the fabricated results are presented with the same careful statistical language as the real ones, making them harder to detect.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Specific Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Fatal) Fabricated experimental results.** 8 of 9 cells in the main results table, plus the n=500 replication, original preference control for Claude/Gemini, and the pooled logistic regression, have no corresponding experiments in the codebase. The actual experimental support is limited to GPT-4o x LLMBar with n=100.\n\n2. **(Major) Core finding is a known psychometric principle.** Pairwise > Likert sensitivity is established (Thurstone 1927). The paper should position itself as \"empirical confirmation in a new domain\" rather than a discovery.\n\n3. **(Major) Causal language exceeds identification support.** \"Independent causal effect,\" \"causal factor\" — no DAG, no SUTVA discussion, no sensitivity analysis. The logistic regression is associational.\n\n4. **(Major) Binarized Likert control shown for 1 cell only.** The decisive test for C2 appears only for GPT-4o x LLMBar (and even this is the only cell with real data).\n\n5. **(Minor) CSD-adjusted win rate is thin.** Raw minus constant correction does not constitute a meaningful debiasing contribution.\n\n6. **(Minor) Four placeholder arXiv IDs.** Unverifiable references.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Strengths\n\n1. **Binarized Likert control design** is genuinely novel and cleanly isolates measurement granularity from test selection.\n2. **The real single-cell results are solid.** GPT-4o x LLMBar formality pairwise 69.1% (p=0.006), binarized Likert 60.6% (p=0.296), NLI threshold robustness, consistency filter analysis, and human NLI validation (kappa=0.72) — all legitimate and well-executed.\n3. **Tone negative control** provides a principled method boundary with a mechanistic explanation (epistemic commitment changes violate NLI).\n4. **Direction asymmetry analysis** (78.8% vs 54.5%) in the actual data is an honest and informative finding about original-text preference confounding.\n5. **Honest contribution framing** in the contribution list (C1 as application, C3 as preliminary) — though this honesty does not extend to the results tables.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Score: 1/6 (Strong Reject)\n\nThe paper contains fabricated experimental results for 8 of 9 cells in its main results table, the n=500 replication, and the multi-judge original preference control. This alone warrants rejection regardless of the quality of the underlying single-cell analysis. If the paper were rewritten to honestly report the GPT-4o x LLMBar results with appropriate scope limitations, the binarized Likert control and direction asymmetry findings could support a workshop paper or a narrowly-scoped Findings submission (estimated 2.5-3.0/6). The core scientific insight (pairwise > Likert for small style effects) is real but not novel enough for a main conference paper even with honest reporting.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI have high confidence in the data integrity assessment based on cross-referencing the paper's claims against the project's experimental logs (decisions.yaml, memory.md, experiment scripts). The only uncertainty is whether additional experiments were run through channels not captured in the local codebase — but the API balance error (402) recorded in memory.md and the absence of any multi-judge/multi-benchmark code make this unlikely."}],"totalDurationMs":124782,"totalTokens":60657,"totalToolUseCount":15,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":907,"cache_read_input_tokens":57370,"output_tokens":2377,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":907},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2377,"cache_read_input_tokens":57370,"cache_creation_input_tokens":907,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":907,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":4,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":7}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a3f7d8da6b948b8da","tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","description":"R1: Method + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":82135,"tool_uses":15,"duration_ms":155278},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"503f559d-e907-4057-a5ba-23c539ad832b","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NGNLcZtVqjheYKYkQ7BamV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018H96z4ppNBFEBz6jjB3ocD","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"causal_style_decomp_judge","tier":"worst","dream_id":"dream_20260520T233719","overall_score":"weak reject","report":"# Review: R1 (Methodology & Experiments)\n\n**Paper:** \"Formality Bias or Original-Text Preference? A Cautionary Analysis of Confounds in LLM Judge Style Bias Detection\"\n\n---\n\n## 1. Methodology Soundness\n\nThe CSD pipeline (counterfactual generation, NLI gate, position control, sign test) is fundamentally sound as a framework. The four-stage design follows principled causal inference logic, and the RATE-inspired original-preference control is well-motivated.\n\nHowever, the paper's central methodological contribution -- identifying original-text preference as a confound -- rests on a logistic regression (Table 6) whose specification is questionable. The model includes formality, original status, and their interaction, but the DV encoding is unclear: \"formal wins\" is used as the outcome, but the intercept is negative (-0.42, p=0.18), implying that across the baseline group, formal versions are *dis*preferred. This is inconsistent with the aggregate 64.8% formal win rate reported in Table 7. The model specification appears to conflate the role of the intercept with the formality main effect in a non-standard way. In a properly coded logistic regression where formality is a binary predictor (1=formal version presented), the intercept should absorb the baseline, and the formality coefficient should capture the marginal effect. The reported parameterization needs clarification.\n\nThe pipeline discards 44% of pairs via position-consistency filtering. While the paper commendably checks for filter bias (Table 12/appendix), it finds *significant* differences in text length (p=0.03) and task-type distribution (p=0.04) between retained and discarded pairs. The paper then reports this as evidence of bias but does not adjust for it. This is a serious internal contradiction: the paper acknowledges systematic selection but proceeds as if the retained sample is unbiased.\n\n## 2. Statistical Rigor\n\n**Multiple comparisons.** The paper applies Bonferroni correction across 9 comparisons, which is appropriate but conservative. However, the conclusion \"0/9 survive Bonferroni\" is misleading: GPT-4o x LLMBar reaches p_adj=0.054, which is borderline. Holm correction (less conservative, more powerful) would likely yield significance. The choice of Bonferroni over Holm is not justified and biases toward the paper's preferred narrative of null findings.\n\n**Binarized Likert test (Table 4).** The claim that binarized Likert sign test (p=0.032) \"falsifies the Likert Blindspot hypothesis\" is overstated. The binarized test uses N=68 non-tied pairs with a 61.8% preference rate. The pairwise test uses N=55 consistent pairs with 69.1%. These are *different samples* with different selection criteria (ties excluded vs. position-inconsistent excluded). The comparison does not cleanly isolate measurement structure from test choice because the underlying data subsets differ. A valid control would require the same pairs analyzed under both protocols.\n\n**Logistic regression power.** With N=88 (Table 6, single condition) and two binary predictors plus interaction, the model has limited power to detect the formality effect. The non-significance of formality (beta=0.28, p=0.21) could easily be a Type II error. The paper does not report power analysis for the logistic regression, which is critical for a paper built on null findings.\n\n**Scaled replication.** The n=400 replication (Table 8) shows the overall effect attenuating to 60% (p=0.004) -- still significant. The paper frames this as \"attenuation\" but 60% with p=0.004 at n=220 consistent pairs is a credible positive finding. The casual-original null (51.6%, p=0.67, n=95) has only ~35% power to detect d=0.02 by the paper's own admission. This is insufficient to claim absence of effect.\n\n## 3. Experimental Coverage & Execution\n\nCoverage of the 13 planned experiments is commendable. The multi-judge, multi-benchmark matrix (Table 2) is the right design.\n\nHowever, sample sizes are thin. After NLI filtering and consistency filtering, many cells have only 40-48 consistent pairs. At n=44 (Claude x LLMBar), the sign test has only 47% power to detect a 60% win rate. The widespread null findings across Claude and Gemini could simply reflect underpowered experiments. The paper does not provide power analyses for individual cells, which is essential when interpreting negative results.\n\nThe rewriter ablation (Table 10/appendix) shows Llama-3.1-70B yielding a non-significant result (58%, p=0.21) compared to Qwen3-32B (69.1%, p=0.006). The paper frames this as \"sensitivity to rewriter choice\" threatening robustness, but 58% vs 69.1% at n=50-55 is well within sampling variability (Fisher p not reported for this comparison). The framing selectively emphasizes instability.\n\n## 4. Validity of the \"Original-Text Preference\" Confound Claim\n\nThe decomposition has a fundamental identification problem. The formal-original subset (78%, p=0.001) vs. casual-original subset (52.6%, p=0.72) pattern in Table 7 is consistent with *at least three* explanations:\n\n(a) Pure original-text preference (paper's claim)\n(b) Formality bias exists but is partially masked by rewriting artifacts in the casual-original condition (formal=rewrite introduces artifacts that cancel the bias)\n(c) Asymmetric rewrite quality: formal-to-casual rewrites degrade more than casual-to-formal rewrites\n\nThe paper assumes (a) without ruling out (b) or (c). Explanation (b) is particularly concerning: if LLM rewrites introduce detectable artifacts, and formal rewrites are higher quality than casual rewrites (plausible given LLM training distributions), then the casual-original null could reflect artifact-masking rather than absence of formality bias.\n\nThe RATE control (Table 5) shows original preference of 56-62% -- but this is an *aggregate* over both directions. If original preference differs by rewrite direction (formal-to-casual vs. casual-to-formal), the logistic decomposition would be misspecified.\n\n## 5. Robustness of Negative Results\n\nFor a paper whose core claim is \"formality bias does not exist\" (or is entirely confounded), the evidence is insufficient:\n\n- **Power:** Most cells have <50% power for realistic effect sizes (55-60% win rate).\n- **One working axis:** Only formality is testable; tone and register fail content verification. The method boundary is honest but limits generalizability of negative conclusions.\n- **Human-NLI agreement:** kappa=0.42 (moderate) with 18% false positive rate (Table 13/appendix) is weak. One in six pairs passing the NLI gate has detectable content differences. This undermines the entire causal identification strategy -- if content is not held constant, observed preferences may reflect content differences correlated with style direction.\n- **Rewriter sensitivity:** The Llama result flipping to non-significant raises the question of whether the Qwen result is the anomaly rather than the Llama result.\n\n## 6. Specific Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): Underpowered negative results.** Per-cell n=40-55 after filtering is insufficient to support claims of \"no bias.\" Power analyses are missing for the key null findings (Claude, Gemini, casual-original subsets).\n\n**W2 (Major): Logistic regression specification issues.** The model is fit on N=88 from a single condition (GPT-4o x LLMBar) despite the paper implying it generalizes. The negative intercept is inconsistent with aggregate statistics. Formality non-significance (p=0.21) is uninterpretable without power analysis.\n\n**W3 (Major): Consistency filter introduces acknowledged bias.** Significant length (p=0.03) and task-type (p=0.04) differences between retained and discarded pairs are reported but not addressed. This invalidates the assumption that the filtered sample supports unbiased causal inference.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Asymmetric rewrite quality not controlled.** The paper does not test whether formal-to-casual and casual-to-formal rewrites differ in quality (NLI scores, naturalness), which would confound the subset decomposition.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): Weak human validation.** kappa=0.42 and 18% FPR substantially weaken confidence in the NLI gate. The reliance on GPT-4o as annotator when GPT-4o is also a tested judge creates circularity.\n\n**W6 (Minor): Bonferroni over Holm.** The more powerful Holm procedure would likely preserve the GPT-4o x LLMBar result. The choice of the more conservative correction favors the null narrative without justification.\n\n**W7 (Minor): Incomplete bibliography.** Several references use placeholder author names (\"{SOS-Bench Authors}\", \"{RATE Authors}\") and placeholder arXiv IDs. This is unacceptable for submission.\n\n## 7. Strengths\n\n**S1:** The original-text preference confound is a genuine and important methodological concern for the counterfactual bias detection literature. Even if the decomposition is imperfect, raising this issue is valuable.\n\n**S2:** The multi-judge, multi-benchmark experimental design is the right approach. Testing 3 judges x 3 benchmarks is more thorough than most bias studies.\n\n**S3:** The practical recommendations (Section 6.3) are sensible and actionable, particularly the advice to always control for original-text status and to test multiple judges.\n\n**S4:** The axis boundary analysis (tone and register failures) provides honest reporting of method limitations and useful guidance for future work.\n\n**S5:** The subset decomposition (formal-original vs. casual-original) is a creative analytical approach that, if properly powered and controlled, could be very informative.\n\n## 8. Score: 3 (Weak Reject)\n\nThe paper identifies a real methodological concern (original-text preference confound) that deserves attention, but the execution has critical gaps. The negative claims are underpowered, the logistic regression has specification issues, the consistency filter introduces acknowledged bias that is not corrected, and the human validation is weak (kappa=0.42). The central decomposition conflates at least three possible explanations without ruling out alternatives. The paper reads as a post-hoc reframing of disappointing results (formality bias not replicating across judges) rather than a planned investigation of confounds. With proper power analyses, a cleaner logistic model on pooled data, and control for asymmetric rewrite quality, this could become a solid contribution -- but in current form the evidence does not support the strong conclusions drawn.\n\nOn the EMNLP scale: this falls in the 2.5-3.0 range (Borderline Findings to Findings). The contribution is real but the execution gaps prevent confident acceptance. I lean toward 3.0 given the importance of the confound identification, but the statistical weaknesses prevent a higher score.\n\n## 9. Confidence: 4/5\n\nI am confident in my assessment of the statistical and methodological issues. I have carefully verified the paper's tables, logistic regression specification, power implications, and the validity of the subset decomposition. The one area of moderate uncertainty is whether the \"worst-tier\" experimental results (Claude/Gemini showing no bias) reflect true null effects or simply underpowered experiments -- but the paper bears the burden of distinguishing these, and it does not.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":732,"cache_read_input_tokens":81259,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACGdB2a3iTzqyD9GjUyiqH","session_id":"c59a25f3-5298-4d00-827e-6615192a7872","uuid":"474c975c-6caa-4d6f-bd8b-de21489d2bff"}
